<div class="Section1">

  <!-- HEADER -->
  <p style="font-size: 9.5pt; font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase; color: #2563eb; margin-bottom: 2pt;">
    Department of Computer Science & Engineering | Software Project Management Lab
  </p>
  <h1>Experiment No. 03: Work Breakdown Structure (WBS), Gantt Chart Scheduling & Project Risk Register</h1>

  <!-- META BOX -->
  <div class="meta-box">
    <table>
      <tr>
        <td style="width: 50%;">
          <strong>Course:</strong> Software Project Management Lab<br>
          <strong>Experiment No.:</strong> 03<br>
          <strong>Date of Experiment:</strong> 01/08/2026<br>
          <strong>Submission Date:</strong> 16/09/2026
        </td>
        <td style="width: 50%;">
          <strong>Project Title:</strong> CareerPilot AI<br>
          <strong>Sub-Title:</strong> Placement Intelligence & Readiness Platform<br>
          <strong>Group Members:</strong><br>
          1. Abel Mathew Bose &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2. K.M. Meenakshi<br>
          3. Sen Shaji &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4. Sidharth K.A
        </td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- 1. AIM -->
  <h2>1. Aim of the Experiment</h2>
  <p>
    1. To create a hierarchical <strong>Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)</strong> and prepare an interactive <strong>Gantt chart</strong> to represent project scheduling using Microsoft Excel.<br>
    2. To formulate a comprehensive <strong>Risk Register</strong> identifying five major operational and technical risks with their probability, impact, risk score ($P \times I$), and mitigation plans.
  </p>

  <!-- 2. THEORY -->
  <h2>2. Theoretical Framework & Principles</h2>
  <p>
    Effective software project engineering requires structured scope definition, transparent timeline tracking, and risk anticipation. This experiment applies three key PMBOK principles:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Work Breakdown Structure (WBS):</strong> A deliverable-oriented hierarchical decomposition of the total project scope. Following the <em>100% Rule</em>, it ensures all terminal work packages completely cover the project boundary without gaps or redundancy.</li>
    <li><strong>Gantt Chart Timeline Scheduling:</strong> A temporal visualization that places each work package along a calendar timeline (16 weekly intervals), illustrating milestones, parallel activities, and completion states.</li>
    <li><strong>Qualitative Risk Analysis:</strong> Systematic risk evaluation based on the mathematical risk score formula:
      <div class="formula-callout">
        Risk Score = Probability (1 – 5) &times; Impact (1 – 5)
      </div>
      Risk scores range from 1 to 25 and classify events into <strong>Critical (16–25)</strong>, <strong>High (10–15)</strong>, <strong>Medium (5–9)</strong>, and <strong>Low (1–4)</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- 3. PROCEDURE -->
  <h2>3. Procedure & Excel Commands</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open Microsoft Excel and create two separate workbooks: <code>CareerPilot_AI_WBS_and_Gantt_Chart.xls</code> and <code>CareerPilot_AI_Risk_Register.xls</code>.</li>
    <li>In the WBS sheet, define the required headers: <code>WBS ID</code>, <code>Phase</code>, <code>Module</code>, <code>Work Package</code>, <code>Description</code>, <code>Owner</code>, <code>Start Date</code>, <code>End Date</code>, <code>Duration (Days)</code>, <code>Status</code>, and <code>Progress</code>.</li>
    <li>Identify the 6 primary phases of CareerPilot AI: Initiation, UI/UX Design, Student Platform Core, AI Opportunity Radar, Skills Trust Engine, and Testing/Deployment.</li>
    <li>Divide the phases into 15 manageable work packages and assign owners across all 4 team members: Abel Mathew Bose, K.M. Meenakshi, Sen Shaji, and Sidharth K.A.</li>
    <li>Enter start and end dates and apply the duration formula:
      <div class="formula-callout">
        =INT(End_Date - Start_Date + 1)  &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;  =H6 - G6 + 1
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>In the <code>Gantt_Chart</code> worksheet, set up a 16-week matrix (W1 to W16) spanning August 1, 2026 to November 21, 2026. Highlight active task cells using status color codes: Completed (Green), In Progress (Blue), and Planned (Gray).</li>
    <li>In the Risk Register workbook, populate 5 major project risks, applying the risk score calculation formula:
      <div class="formula-callout">
        =Probability * Impact  &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;  =E6 * F6
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

